% ================================================================

\begin{definition}[Vacuum Frequency]
The \textbf{Vacuum Frequency} $\nuvac$ is the propagation frequency of a gauge boson of
physical height $\lq$ travelling at $c$ in a perfect vacuum. It is the number of gauge boson
lengths that fit within one light-second:
\begin{equation}
  \nuvac = \frac{c}{\lq}
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

The vacuum frequency is categorically distinct from the zero-point oscillation frequency
$\nuz$. Two kinds of frequency exist in this framework:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em]
  \item \textbf{Internal oscillation frequency} $\nuz$: the rate at which a gauge boson
        completes one cycle of its own electromagnetic oscillation. For a zero-point boson,
        $\nuz = 1\,\mathrm{Hz}$.
  \item \textbf{Propagation frequency} $\nuvac$: the rate at which gauge bosons of physical
        length $\lq$ pass a fixed point when travelling at $c$. This is a counting frequency,
        not an oscillation frequency.
\end{enumerate}

The conflation of these two frequencies in standard quantum field theory is, within this
framework, the source of the apparent paradox whereby a $1\,\mathrm{Hz}$ quantum would
have a wavelength of $299{,}792{,}458\,\mathrm{m}$ --- larger than the Earth --- while
neutrinos with fascia of $1\,\mathrm{Hz}$ are detected as point-like particles in underground
detectors. The resolution is that $1\,\mathrm{Hz}$ is the \textit{internal} frequency of the
boson, while its \textit{physical} size is determined by $\lq$, and its propagation rate by
$\nuvac$.

