\section{The Equilateral Gauge: Geometric Necessity of $\sqrt{3}$}
% ================================================================

\begin{theorem}[Geometric Necessity of the Equilateral Quantum]
The equilateral triangle with height $\lq$ is the unique planar polygon consistent with all
of the following simultaneously:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
  \item The Pythagorean law: $(L/2)^{2} + \lq^{2} = L^{2}$
  \item Perfect planar tessellation without gaps or overlaps
  \item Dimensional conservation of Planck's action $h = \mathrm{kg} \cdot m^{2}/s$
  \item The mass-energy relation $h = \mz \cdot c^{2}$
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
From the Pythagorean law for the equilateral triangle:
\begin{equation}
  \lq^{2} = L^{2} - \frac{L^{2}}{4} = \frac{3L^{2}}{4}
  \qquad \Longrightarrow \qquad
  L = \frac{2}{\sqrt{3}}\lq
\end{equation}
This ratio is unique to the equilateral triangle. The gauge area is therefore:
\begin{equation}
  \Aq = \frac{1}{2} \cdot L \cdot \lq
  = \frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{2}{\sqrt{3}}\lq \cdot \lq
  = \frac{\lq^{2}}{\sqrt{3}}
  \qquad \Longrightarrow \qquad
  \lq^{2} = \sqrt{3}\,\Aq
\end{equation}
Expressing $c^{2}$ geometrically via $c = \lq/s$:
\begin{equation}
  c^{2} = \frac{\lq^{2}}{s^{2}} = \frac{\sqrt{3}\,\Aq}{s^{2}}
\end{equation}
Substituting into $h = \mz \cdot c^{2} \cdot T$:
\begin{equation}
  h = \mz \cdot \frac{\sqrt{3}\,\Aq}{s^{2}} \cdot T
\end{equation}
For any other polygon, the height-to-side ratio differs from $\sqrt{3}/2$:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Square} (90°): ratio $= 1$, giving $h = \mz \cdot \lq^{2}/s^{2} = \mz \cdot c^{2}$,
        which has units of joules, not joule-seconds. \textit{Dimensional failure.}
  \item \textbf{Right isoceles} (45°-45°-90°): ratio $= \sqrt{2}/2$, giving
        $h = \mz \cdot \sqrt{2}\Aq/s^{2}$, which deviates from the measured $h$ by factor
        $\sqrt{2}/\sqrt{3}$. \textit{Conservation failure.}
  \item \textbf{General triangle} ($\theta \neq 60°$): $2\sin\theta \neq \sqrt{3}$, breaking
        the numerical value of $h$. \textit{Conservation failure.}
\end{itemize}
Only $\theta = 60°$ produces the $\sqrt{3}$ factor that conserves $h$ dimensionally and
numerically. Furthermore, only the equilateral triangle tiles a flat plane with six faces at
each vertex summing to $360°$. No other triangle achieves this with a single tile type.
\end{proof}

